.TH FSTAB 5 "November 2026" "LikeOS" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
fstab \- the file systems to mount
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/fstab
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B /etc/fstab
lists the file systems of the computer and where they are mounted.
.BR init (8)
reads it at start-up and mounts them;
.BR mount (1)
and
.BR umount (1)
read it to complete a command that names only a mount point or a device,
and
.B mount \-a
mounts what it lists.
.PP
The file is plain text, one file system per line.
Empty lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
A line has six fields, separated by blanks (spaces or tabs):
.PP
.RS
.I fs_spec  fs_file  fs_vfstype  fs_mntops  fs_freq  fs_passno
.RE
.PP
A blank inside a field is written
.B \e040
(a tab
.BR \e011 ,
a backslash
.BR \e134 ).
The last two fields may be left out; they are 0 then.
.SS fs_spec
What is mounted:
.TP
.BI UUID= uuid
The file system with this UUID (ext4, or the
.IR xxxx\-xxxx
volume serial of a FAT file system), as
.BR blkid (8)
shows it.
The best choice: it stays the same when disks are added, removed or found
in another order.
.TP
.BI LABEL= label
The file system with this label
.RB ( e2label (8),
.BR fatlabel (8)).
.TP
.BI PARTUUID= uuid
The partition with this unique partition GUID (GPT) or MBR identifier
.RI ( ssssssss\-nn ).
.TP
.BI PARTLABEL= name
The partition with this name (GPT).
.TP
.BI /dev/ device
A device by name:
.BR /dev/sda1 ,
.BR /dev/nvme0n1p2 .
Names follow the order in which disks are found, and can change.
.TP
.B tmpfs
For a file system without a device.
.PP
The tags are looked up in
.BR /dev/disk/by\-uuid ,
.BR by\-label ,
.B by\-partuuid
and
.BR by\-partlabel .
At start-up a device that is not there yet is waited for (up to ten
seconds: USB and NVMe disks come up after
.B init
starts).
.SS fs_file
The mount point: an absolute path.
At start-up
.B init
makes it when it does not exist;
.B mount
does that only for a line with
.BR X\-mount.mkdir .
.SS fs_vfstype
The file system type:
.TP
.B ext4
The LikeOS root file system and data disks (ext2 and ext3 file systems are
mounted as ext4).
.TP
.B vfat
FAT12, FAT16 and FAT32: the EFI system partition, USB sticks, memory cards
.RB ( fat ,
.B fat32
and
.B msdos
mean the same).
.TP
.B tmpfs
A file system in memory.
.TP
.B auto
Whatever the kernel recognises on the device.
.TP
.B swap
Not mounted; the line is passed over.
.SS fs_mntops
The mount options, separated by commas, as for
.BR mount (1);
.B defaults
when there are none to give.
The generic ones:
.TP
.B defaults
.BR rw ,
.BR suid ,
.BR dev ,
.BR exec ,
.BR auto ,
.BR async .
.TP
.BR ro ", " rw
Read-only, read-write.
.TP
.B noauto
Not mounted at start-up nor by
.BR "mount \-a" ;
only on request
.RB ( "mount /media/stick" ).
.TP
.B nofail
A device that is not there is no error: nothing is said at start-up.
.TP
.BR nosuid ", " nodev ", " noexec
No set-user-ID programs, no device files, no programs run from the file
system.
.TP
.BR noatime ", " relatime ", " strictatime ", " nodiratime
When access times are updated.
.TP
.BR sync ", " dirsync ", " lazytime ", " nosymfollow
See
.BR mount (1).
.TP
.BR X\-mount.mkdir [ =\fImode\fR ]
Make the mount point, with this mode (octal, default 0755).
.PP
The file system's own options:
.TP
.B vfat
.BI uid= n\fR,
.BI gid= n
(the owner every file shows),
.BI umask= octal\fR,
.BI fmask= octal\fR,
.BI dmask= octal
(the permission bits taken away from everything, from files, from
directories; without them files are 0644 and directories 0755 and anyone
may read and write),
.BR shortname= lower | win95 | winnt | mixed .
.TP
.B tmpfs
.BI size= n
(bytes with a
.BR k ,
.B m
or
.B g
suffix, or a percentage of the memory with
.BR % ;
default: a quarter of the memory),
.BI nr_inodes= n\fR,
.BI mode= octal
(of the root directory, default 1777),
.BI uid= n\fR,
.BI gid= n\fR.
.TP
.B ext4
None.
.SS fs_freq
The dump frequency.
Kept for compatibility; nothing on LikeOS reads it.
Write 0.
.SS fs_passno
Whether and in which order the file system is checked at start-up, before
it is mounted:
.B 0
not checked,
.B 1
first,
.B 2
after those with 1, and so on.
The root file system gets 1, the others 2 (or 0).
The checker is
.BR fsck.ext4 " \-p"
(that is,
.BR e2fsck (8))
for ext4 and
.BR fsck.vfat " \-a"
.RB ( fsck.fat (8))
for FAT; a tmpfs is never checked.
A file system with errors the checker could not repair is mounted
read-only, and the console says so.
.SH THE ROOT FILE SYSTEM
The root file system is mounted by the kernel before
.B init
runs; its line
.RB ( fs_file
.BR / )
is not mounted again.
When the line asks for other flags than the root has
.RB ( ro ,
.BR noatime ...)
the root is remounted with them, and a root mounted read-only is checked
first when its
.I fs_passno
is above 0.
.SH START-UP
At start-up
.BR init (8)
goes through the file the way
.B mount \-a
does: lines marked
.B noauto
and swap lines, and mount points something is mounted on already, are
passed over; the devices are looked up (and waited for); the file systems
with a pass number above 0 are checked, pass 1 before pass 2; then the
lines are mounted in the order of the file.
Every step is bounded in time: a device that never appears, a check that
never finishes or a mount that hangs costs that one file system, never the
start-up.
.SH EXAMPLES
The file the LikeOS installer writes, with the root and the EFI system
partition named by their UUIDs:
.PP
.RS
.nf
UUID=6c1f...9d2a /         ext4 defaults                   0 1
UUID=3A7B\-91C4   /boot/efi vfat umask=0077,shortname=mixed 0 2
.fi
.RE
.PP
A USB stick mounted on request, owned by user 1000:
.PP
.RS
.nf
LABEL=STICK /media/stick vfat noauto,nofail,uid=1000,gid=1000 0 0
.fi
.RE
.PP
A further memory file system for scratch files (the kernel itself mounts
/ram, the one luakit uses):
.PP
.RS
.nf
tmpfs  /scratch  tmpfs  size=512m,mode=1777,nosuid,nodev  0  0
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/fstab
The file.
.TP
.B /proc/mounts
What is mounted now, in the same format.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (1),
.BR umount (1),
.BR blkid (8),
.BR e2fsck (8),
.BR fsck.fat (8),
.BR init (8)
